The C program is small — read one int with [38;5;10mscanf("%d", &x)[0m, then loop [38;5;10mi[0m from 0 while [38;5;10mi < x[0m, printing [38;5;10mi[0m and a parallel counter [38;5;10mj[0m that steps by 2. The translation keeps the two-variable comma-loop structure rather than computing [38;5;10m2*i[0m, so overflow behavior stays the same.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two details that mattered for exactness:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **Failed [38;5;10mscanf[0m.** C leaves [38;5;10mx[0m at its initialized 0 when no conversion happens (empty input, whitespace only, [38;5;10mabc[0m, a bare [38;5;10m-[0m). My [38;5;10mscan_i32[0m returns 0 without touching the out-param, so those inputs print nothing.[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10m%d[0m semantics.** Leading whitespace is skipped across newlines, an optional sign is accepted, and at least one digit is required — a non-digit is pushed back. On overflow glibc saturates at [38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m and then narrows to [38;5;10mint[0m, so [38;5;10m99999999999[0m and [38;5;10m4294967301[0m produce the same truncated values as the C build. Input is read a byte at a time through a one-byte pushback slot so the stream is never advanced past what the conversion consumes.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One caveat on scope: the saturate-then-truncate path reproduces what glibc does on this x86-64 Linux host, which is what I tested against. C leaves [38;5;10m%d[0m overflow unspecified, so a different libc could disagree there; every non-overflow input is portable-exact.[0m[0m
